    "narrative": "The Houston metro's large, young, and heavily Hispanic population creates one of the nation's most fertile markets for Spanish-English immersion early education.\n\nThe Houston-Pasadena-The Woodlands metro area, covering roughly 7.4 million residents across 10 geographic areas, represents an exceptionally strong environment for Dos Idiomas Early Learning Center. The region combines two demographic forces that align perfectly with a bilingual immersion model: a robust base of young children and a deep, culturally rooted Hispanic community. Children under age 10 make up between 11.1% and 16.4% of the population depending on the area—a sizable and geographically concentrated pool of potential enrollees for daycare, preschool, and after-school programming. This youthful family structure provides both immediate enrollment demand and a durable pipeline of future students.\n\nWhat distinguishes Houston from a generic childcare market is the natural, organic demand for bilingual education. Hispanic residents represent 28.5% to 42.6% of the population across the metro, and 3.0% to 9.8% of households have limited English proficiency. This is not a market where immersion must be \"sold\" as a novelty—it is a market where Spanish-English fluency is a lived family value and a practical necessity. That dynamic supports strong demand for both the core immersion preschool and the parent language workshops, which can serve as a differentiating community anchor and a lead-generation channel for full enrollment.\n\nThe economic and workforce indicators reinforce the opportunity. Median household incomes range from $69K to $89K, and college attainment spans 9.5% to 36.3%, signaling clear opportunities for a tiered pricing and scholarship strategy that captures both premium-positioned immersion families and value-conscious working households. Labor force participation of 53.1% to 68.4% points to widespread need for full-day and after-school care, while home ownership rates of 57.1% to 82.9% indicate settled, permanent neighborhoods that correlate with longer enrollment retention—an important factor for site selection and long-term center profitability.",
